  "reasoning": "The product contains a benzene ring substituted with a methoxy group, a ring bromine, and a benzylic bromomethyl group (-CH2Br). In Functional Group Addition (FGA) reactions of this type, a C-Br bond on an alkyl side chain is formed by brominating a corresponding C-H (methyl) group. Following the established pattern in the training references (Refs 1-4), the brominating agent is N-bromosuccinimide, represented by the SMILES O=C1CCC(=O)N1Br. Disconnecting the benzylic bromine reveals the precursor arene where the -CH2Br group is replaced by a methyl group (SMILES: C). The ring bromine is retained from the starting material, consistent with selective benzylic bromination seen in Ref 1. Therefore, the precursors are the methyl-substituted arene and the brominating agent.",
  "final_answer": "COc1ccc(Br)c(C)c1.O=C1CCC(=O)N1Br"
}

